:68.7},{"z":"Blaenau Gwent","x":"2020","y":71},{"z":"Blaenau Gwent","x":"2021","y":70.1},{"z":"Blaenau Gwent","x":"2022","y":73.8},{"z":"Blaenau Gwent","x":"2023","y":69.4},{"z":"Torfaen","x":"2013","y":66.4},{"z":"Torfaen","x":"2014","y":68.6},{"z":"Torfaen","x":"2015","y":71.3},{"z":"Torfaen","x":"2016","y":75},{"z":"Torfaen","x":"2017","y":75.7},{"z":"Torfaen","x":"2018","y":70.5},{"z":"Torfaen","x":"2019","y":71.5},{"z":"Torfaen","x":"2020","y":72},{"z":"Torfaen","x":"2021","y":69.8},{"z":"Torfaen","x":"2022","y":73.3},{"z":"Torfaen","x":"2023","y":76.4},{"z":"Flintshire","x":"2013","y":72.5},{"z":"Flintshire","x":"2014","y":71.2},{"z":"Flintshire","x":"2015","y":73.5},{"z":"Flintshire","x":"2016","y":72.6},{"z":"Flintshire","x":"2017","y":76.1},{"z":"Flintshire","x":"2018","y":77.3},{"z":"Flintshire","x":"2019","y":79.4},{"z":"Flintshire","x":"2020","y":79.1},{"z":"Flintshire","x":"2021","y":78.4},{"z":"Flintshire","x":"2022","y":77.8},{"z":"Flintshire","x":"2023","y":78.5},{"z":"The Vale of Glamorgan","x":"2013","y":71.8},{"z":"The Vale of Glamorgan","x":"2014","y":71.8},{"z":"The Vale of Glamorgan","x":"2015","y":73.4},{"z":"The Vale of Glamorgan","x":"2016","y":73.1},{"z":"The Vale of Glamorgan","x":"2017","y":75.2},{"z":"The Vale of Glamorgan","x":"2018","y":78.5},{"z":"The Vale of Glamorgan","x":"2019","y":76.4},{"z":"The Vale of Glamorgan","x":"2020","y":72.9},{"z":"The Vale of Glamorgan","x":"2021","y":77.5},{"z":"The Vale of Glamorgan","x":"2022","y":79.1},{"z":"The Vale of Glamorgan","x":"2023","y":77.5},{"z":"Newport","x":"2013","y":67.9},{"z":"Newport","x":"2014","y":69.8},{"z":"Newport","x":"2015","y":69},{"z":"Newport","x":"2016","y":71},{"z":"Newport","x":"2017","y":75.5},{"z":"Newport","x":"2018","y":74.6},{"z":"Newport","x":"2019","y":73.8},{"z":"Newport","x":"2020","y":75.4},{"z":"Newport","x":"2021","y":72.4},{"z":"Newport","x":"2022","y":72.9},{"z":"Newport","x":"2023","y":72.7}],"selected":"Conwy","padding-top":"20","padding-left":"35","padding-right":"100","padding-bottom":"28","yMin":"40","ySuffix":"%","note":"Figures are based on a small number of survey responses in each area and are less precise than national or regional estimates. Ranking, and changes in ranking, may be the result of sampling variability."},{"id":"employment-level","content":"<p>Overall, there were around 51,800 people aged 16 and over in Conwy who were employed in the year ending December 2023. This is up from the previous year, when there were around 51,600 people aged 16 and over who were employed.</p><p>Local population changes mean that the numbers of people in each area who are employed, unemployed and economically inactive in this article may be under- or over-estimates.</p>"},{"type":"end","content":"<p><a href=\"#section-top\">Back to top</a></p>"},{"id":"unemployment","title":"Unemployment rose in Conwy compared with the previous year","content":"<p>Unemployment estimates are modelled to improve their precision compared to those based only on responses provided via the Annual Population Survey.</p><p>Around 1,800 people aged 16 and over in Conwy were unemployed in the year ending December 2023. This is a rate of 3.3%.</p><p>This was an increase compared with the year ending  December 2022 when the unemployment rate was 2.7%.</p><p>Across Wales, from the year ending  December 2022 to the year ending December 2023, there was an increase in the unemployment rate from 3.0% to 3.7%.</p><p>Year on year, the number of people unemployed in Wales rose from around 45,400 to around 57,300 over the same period.</p><p>Unemployment across Great Britain rose slightly between the year ending December 2022 and the year ending  December 2023, going from around 1,180,000 people (3.6%) to around 1,240,000 (3.7%).</p><p>Unemployed people are those without jobs who are actively seeking work and available to take up a job. Some jobless people may not seek work or be able to work for various reasons, such as retirement, sickness, or study, and are called \"economically inactive\". </p><p>This means that the employment rate and the unemployment rate can both go up at the same time as people move into the labour market and become job seekers.</p><p>The unemployment rate is the number of unemployed people out of everyone \"economically active\" in the labour market, that is, everyone who is employed or looking for work. The employment rate is out of the working-age population including  \"economically inactive\" people. </p>"},{"id":"chart-highest","type":"Chart","chartType":"bar-highlight","title":"Highest unemployment rates (%) of areas in Wales, with Conwy for comparison","subtitle":"Among those aged 16 and over and active in the labour market, year ending December 2023","footer":"Source: Model-based estimates of unemployment from the Office for National Statistics","data":[{"x":5,"y":"Cardiff"},{"x":4.1,"y":"Blaenau Gwent"},{"x":4.1,"y":"Newport"},{"x":3.8,"y":"Isle of Anglesey"},{"x":3.8,"y":"Carmarthenshire"},{"x":3.3,"y":"Conwy"}],"selected":"Conwy","padding-left":"120","xSuffix":"%"},{"type":"end","content":"<p><a href=\"#section-top\">Back to top</a></p>"},{"id":"claimant-count","title":"Claimant Count in Conwy was around the same as in the previous year","content":"<p>The <a href=\"https://www.ons.gov.uk/employmentandlabourmarket/peopleinwork/employmentandemployeetypes/methodologies/aguidetolabourmarketstatistics#claimant-count\" rel=\"external\">Claimant Count</a> is an experimental statistic that measures the number of people who are claiming unemployment-related benefits.</p><p>The Claimant Count in Conwy was 2495 in March 2024, up from March 2023, when there were 2395 claimants.</p><p>The proportion of people aged 16 to 64 years in Conwy who were claiming unemployment-related benefits was 3.8% in March 2024, a similar value compared with March 2023 when it was 3.7%.</p>"},{"id":"chart-claimanttime","type":"Chart","chartType":"line-highlight","title":"Claimant Count (%) for areas in Wales","sub